IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Judiciary Act 1903 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what a person with a civil claim meets when the other side is the
-- Commonwealth or a State, or when the claim is in federal jurisdiction: where I can sue the Commonwealth (s 56) or a State
-- (s 58); whom I must serve (s 63); whether my rights and costs are as against a private person (s 64); which State's or
-- Territory's law binds the court (s 79(1), (1A)(b), (2)-(4), s 80A); which court can review a Commonwealth officer's decision
-- by mandamus, prohibition or injunction (s 39B); when the court must wait for the Attorneys-General to be told of a
-- constitutional point (ss 78AA, 78B); interest up to judgment and on the judgment debt in the High Court (ss 77MA, 77N);
-- remittal of a High Court suit (s 44(1), (2A)); how a judgment against the Commonwealth or a State is paid, and why I cannot
-- execute it (ss 65, 66, 67E); and, on the answering side, where the Commonwealth can sue me for a penalty or a tax (ss 82,
-- 83), how it can enforce a judgment against me (s 67), and who cannot be sued for acting under a Legal Services Direction
-- (ss 55ZG(3), 55ZI).
--
-- SEAT: the claimant -- an individual with a civil claim (in contract, in tort, for review of an official's decision, to
-- recover an invalid tax) against the Commonwealth, a State or a Commonwealth officer, or in a suit in federal jurisdiction;
-- and the same individual when the Commonwealth sues them. "Where can I sue the Commonwealth for my injury? Whom do I serve?
-- Can I get costs? How do I get paid?" Provisions addressed to courts, Registrars, Ministers and Treasurers are encoded as what
-- they mean for me. Every finding or satisfaction the Act leaves to a court is asked as an input, never guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2024C00864 (Compilation No. 51, compilation date 11 December 2024; Federal Register
-- of Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C1903A00006.txt.
--
-- BESIDE, NOT INSTEAD OF: ../baseline-2026-10/ encodes the accused's seat (ss 68, 68A, 79, 80, criminal side). Nothing in it is
-- changed or imported; this module keeps its own names.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: see NOTES.md (Scope). In short: the High Court's own procedure and appellate jurisdiction (Parts III-V except
-- s 44), removal (ss 40-43, 45), Part VIII, the practitioners and AGS provisions of Part VIIIA (except ss 55ZG(3), 55ZI), ss 57,
-- 59-62 (suits by or between governments), Part IXA except s 67E, Part X (criminal; see the accused's seat), ss 77M, 77P-77V
-- (High Court enforcement detail, suppression and vexatious proceedings orders), s 78A, s 80 (encoded in the accused's seat),
-- ss 81, 84-88 and the Schedule's form beyond naming it.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the claimant's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Judiciary Act 1903 -- the claimant (a civil claim against the Commonwealth or a State, or in federal jurisdiction)`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with a `Claim answer`: either `answered` with the
-- value, or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why this is not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 56(1): "A person making a claim against the Commonwealth"
    `my claim is not against the Commonwealth, so s 56 does not say where to bring it`
    -- s 56(1), s 58: "whether in contract or in tort"
    `my claim is neither in contract nor in tort; s 56 and s 58 name claims "in contract or in tort", and other routes (s 39B, s 39(2), the Constitution) are not worked out here`
    -- s 58: "Any person making any claim against a State ... in respect of a matter in which the High Court has original jurisdiction or can have original jurisdiction conferred on it"
    `my claim is not against a State in a matter of federal jurisdiction, so s 58 does not apply; the State's own law on suing the State governs it, and that is not encoded here`
    -- s 63: "Where the Commonwealth or a State is a Party"; s 65, s 66: "the Commonwealth or a State"
    `the government party is a Territory, not the Commonwealth or a State; ss 63, 65 and 66 name only the Commonwealth and the States`
    -- s 39B(2)
    `a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) is not an "officer of the Commonwealth" for s 39B(1) and (1B)-(1D); the route to review a Judge's decision is not encoded here`
    -- s 79(1), s 80A
    `the court is not exercising federal jurisdiction, and it is not a Territory court hearing a s 56 suit, so s 79 does not apply; the law of the place applies of its own force`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Claim answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why this is not covered`

§§ `The States and Territories, the governments, and the actors`

-- "each State or Territory" (s 79(1)); "the Supreme Court of that State or Territory" (s 56(1)(b)). The Territories are named as
-- the Act names them; an external Territory is a Territory.
DECLARE `Place` IS ONE OF
    `New South Wales`
    `Victoria`
    `Queensland`
    `Western Australia`
    `South Australia`
    `Tasmania`
    `the Australian Capital Territory`
    `the Northern Territory`
    `another Territory (such as Norfolk Island or the Jervis Bay Territory)`

-- The six original States. The Act's "State" means these, except in Division 1A of Part XI, where s 78AA adds the Australian
-- Capital Territory and the Northern Territory.
GIVEN p IS A `Place`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is one of the six States` p IF
        p EQUALS `New South Wales`
    OR  p EQUALS `Victoria`
    OR  p EQUALS `Queensland`
    OR  p EQUALS `Western Australia`
    OR  p EQUALS `South Australia`
    OR  p EQUALS `Tasmania`

-- The government that is a party to my suit, or that I hold a judgment against.
DECLARE `Government` IS ONE OF
    `the Commonwealth`
    `the government of` HAS `which` IS A `Place`

-- The parties and acts the regulative rules below name. I am the person in the seat.
DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the claimant`
    `the court`
    `the Registrar or other appropriate officer`
    `the Minister for Finance`
    `the Treasurer of the State`

-- s 63: those on whom process may be served.
DECLARE `Person served` IS ONE OF
    `the Attorney-General of that government`
    `a person that Attorney-General has appointed to receive service`
    `someone else (such as the department, the agency, the Minister or the Crown Solicitor without an appointment)`

-- s 78B(2)(c), (5): what the court does in the cause.
DECLARE `Step in my case` IS ONE OF
    `grant urgent interlocutory relief that the court thinks necessary in the interests of justice`
    `hear evidence and argument on matters severable from the constitutional point`
    `anything else (go on with the case)`

DECLARE `Claim act` IS ONE OF
    -- s 63: serving process in the suit
    `serve process on` HAS `whom` IS A `Person served`
    -- s 65: "execution or attachment, or process in the nature thereof"
    `issue execution or attachment against the property or revenues of` HAS `whose` IS A `Government`
    -- s 65: "a certificate in the form of the Schedule, or to a like effect"
    `give me a certificate of the judgment`
    -- s 66: "satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available"
    `satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available` HAS `amount` IS A NUMBER
    -- s 78B(1): "proceed in the cause"
    `proceed in the cause` HAS `satisfied notice has been given` IS A BOOLEAN
                               `satisfied a reasonable time has elapsed` IS A BOOLEAN
                               `the step` IS A `Step in my case`

§§ `s 56(1) -- where can I sue the Commonwealth (if your claim is against the Commonwealth)`

-- s 56(1): "A person making a claim against the Commonwealth, whether in contract or in tort, may in respect of the claim bring
-- a suit against the Commonwealth: (a) in the High Court; (b) if the claim arose in a State or Territory -- in the Supreme Court
-- of that State or Territory or in any other court of competent jurisdiction of that State or Territory; or (c) if the claim
-- did not arise in a State or Territory -- in the Supreme Court of any State or Territory or in any other court of competent
-- jurisdiction of any State or Territory."
DECLARE `Courts for my suit against the Commonwealth` IS ONE OF
    `the High Court (s 56(1)(a)), or the Supreme Court or any other court of competent jurisdiction of the State or Territory where my claim arose (s 56(1)(b))` HAS `where` IS A `Place`
    `the High Court (s 56(1)(a)), or the Supreme Court or any other court of competent jurisdiction of any State or Territory (s 56(1)(c))`

@export I have a claim against the Commonwealth (for example for an injury, a breach of contract or a negligent act of a Commonwealth agency). In which courts can I sue the Commonwealth?
GIVEN `my claim is against the Commonwealth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party you are claiming against is the Commonwealth itself (a department or an agency that is part of the Commonwealth), not a separate company or a State
      `my claim is in contract or in tort` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for a breach of contract or for a wrong such as negligence, trespass, nuisance or defamation (a claim in tort)
      `my claim arose in a State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the events giving rise to your claim happened in an Australian State or Territory (answer no if they happened overseas or at sea outside every State and Territory)
      `the State or Territory where my claim arose` IS A `Place` @desc If your claim arose in a State or Territory, which one (otherwise any value; it is not read)
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Courts for my suit against the Commonwealth`
`Where can I sue the Commonwealth question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my claim is against the Commonwealth`)
    THEN `not covered` `my claim is not against the Commonwealth, so s 56 does not say where to bring it`
    ELSE IF (NOT `my claim is in contract or in tort`)
         THEN `not covered` `my claim is neither in contract nor in tort; s 56 and s 58 name claims "in contract or in tort", and other routes (s 39B, s 39(2), the Constitution) are not worked out here`
         ELSE IF `my claim arose in a State or Territory`
              THEN `answered` (`the High Court (s 56(1)(a)), or the Supreme Court or any other court of competent jurisdiction of the State or Territory where my claim arose (s 56(1)(b))` `the State or Territory where my claim arose`)
              ELSE `answered` `the High Court (s 56(1)(a)), or the Supreme Court or any other court of competent jurisdiction of any State or Territory (s 56(1)(c))`

§§ `s 56(2) -- is this court a court of competent jurisdiction for my suit against the Commonwealth`

-- s 56(2): "(a) any court exercising jurisdiction at any place in the capital city of a State, or in the principal or only city
-- or town of a Territory, that would be competent to hear the suit if the Commonwealth were, or had at any time been, resident in
-- that city or town, or in a particular area in that city or town, is a court of competent jurisdiction; and (b) any other court
-- is not a court of competent jurisdiction if its competence to hear the suit would depend upon the place where the Commonwealth
-- resides or carries on business or at any time resided or carried on business."
-- "court of competent jurisdiction" is otherwise not defined: whether the court is competent apart from the Commonwealth's
-- residence (its subject-matter and money limits under State or Territory law) is asked as a fact.
-- FORK (s 56(1)(b), (c); `Is this court competent for my suit against the Commonwealth question`): a court that is not competent
-- on any ground answers JUST FALSE, read as the plain meaning of "competent"; the other reading is NOTHING, the term being
-- left to State and Territory law.
@export I want to sue the Commonwealth in a particular State or Territory court (for example a District Court or a Magistrates Court). Is it a court of competent jurisdiction for my suit?
GIVEN `the court sits in a State capital or the main city of a Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is sitting in the capital city of a State (such as Perth or Sydney), or in the principal or only city or town of a Territory (such as Canberra or Darwin)
      `the court would be competent if the Commonwealth lived there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court could hear your suit if the Commonwealth were (or had been) resident in that city or town, or in the court's area of it
      `the court's competence depends on where the Commonwealth lives or does business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can hear your suit only because of where the defendant resides or carries on business (or once did), rather than because of where the claim arose or its kind and amount
      `the court is competent on other grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court could hear a claim of this kind and amount anyway, for a reason other than where the defendant resides or carries on business (for example because the claim arose in its area and is within its money limit)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this court competent for my suit against the Commonwealth question` MEANS
    IF `the court sits in a State capital or the main city of a Territory` AND `the court would be competent if the Commonwealth lived there`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `the court's competence depends on where the Commonwealth lives or does business`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `the court is competent on other grounds`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 58 -- where can I sue a State (if your claim is against a State in a federal matter)`

-- s 58: "Any person making any claim against a State, whether in contract or in tort, in respect of a matter in which the High
-- Court has original jurisdiction or can have original jurisdiction conferred on it, may in respect of the claim bring a suit
-- against the State in the Supreme Court of the State, or (if the High Court has original jurisdiction in the matter) in the High
-- Court." Which matters those are is set by ss 75 and 76 of the Constitution (not deposited) and s 30 of this Act; asked as facts.
DECLARE `Courts for my suit against a State` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court of that State, or the High Court (s 58)` HAS `which State` IS A `Place`
    `the Supreme Court of that State (s 58); not the High Court, which does not have original jurisdiction in the matter` HAS `which State` IS A `Place`

@export I have a claim against a State in a matter of federal jurisdiction (for example I live in another State, or my claim arises under a Commonwealth law or the Constitution). In which courts can I sue the State?
GIVEN `the State I am claiming against` IS A `Place` @desc Which State you are claiming against (one of the six States; a Territory is not a State here)
      `my claim is in contract or in tort` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for a breach of contract or for a wrong such as negligence (a claim in tort)
      `the High Court has original jurisdiction in the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the High Court can already hear the matter at first instance (for example a claim by a resident of one State against another State, or a matter arising under the Constitution)
      `original jurisdiction in the matter can be conferred on the High Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is one in which the Parliament could give the High Court original jurisdiction (for example a matter arising under a Commonwealth law), even if it has not
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Courts for my suit against a State`
`Where can I sue a State question` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`is one of the six States` `the State I am claiming against`))
       OR ((NOT `the High Court has original jurisdiction in the matter`) AND (NOT `original jurisdiction in the matter can be conferred on the High Court`))
    THEN `not covered` `my claim is not against a State in a matter of federal jurisdiction, so s 58 does not apply; the State's own law on suing the State governs it, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT `my claim is in contract or in tort`)
         THEN `not covered` `my claim is neither in contract nor in tort; s 56 and s 58 name claims "in contract or in tort", and other routes (s 39B, s 39(2), the Constitution) are not worked out here`
         ELSE IF `the High Court has original jurisdiction in the matter`
              THEN `answered` (`the Supreme Court of that State, or the High Court (s 58)` `the State I am claiming against`)
              ELSE `answered` (`the Supreme Court of that State (s 58); not the High Court, which does not have original jurisdiction in the matter` `the State I am claiming against`)

§§ `s 63 -- whom must I serve (if you are suing the Commonwealth or a State)`

-- s 63: "Where the Commonwealth or a State is a Party to a suit, all process in the suit required to be served upon that party
-- shall be served upon the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of the State, as the case may be, or upon some person
-- appointed by him or her to receive service."
DECLARE `Whom I serve` IS ONE OF
    `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth, or a person the Attorney-General has appointed to receive service (s 63)`
    `the Attorney-General of that State, or a person that Attorney-General has appointed to receive service (s 63)` HAS `which State` IS A `Place`

@export I am suing the Commonwealth or a State. On whom must I serve the documents in my suit?
GIVEN `the government I am suing` IS A `Government` @desc The government that is a party to your suit: the Commonwealth, or the government of a State or Territory (name which)
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Whom I serve`
`Whom must I serve question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the government I am suing`
    WHEN `the Commonwealth` THEN `answered` `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth, or a person the Attorney-General has appointed to receive service (s 63)`
    WHEN `the government of` p THEN
        IF `is one of the six States` p
        THEN `answered` (`the Attorney-General of that State, or a person that Attorney-General has appointed to receive service (s 63)` p)
        ELSE `not covered` `the government party is a Territory, not the Commonwealth or a State; ss 63, 65 and 66 name only the Commonwealth and the States`

-- s 63 as a duty on me (the party serving). "shall be served upon" sets no time: the duty waits until process is served on
-- the Attorney-General or the appointed person. Serving someone else does not discharge it.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 63 -- I must serve process on the Attorney-General or the person appointed to receive service` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    MUST  `serve process on` whom PROVIDED (whom EQUALS `the Attorney-General of that government` OR whom EQUALS `a person that Attorney-General has appointed to receive service`)
    HENCE FULFILLED

§§ `s 64 -- are my rights and costs as against a private person (if the Commonwealth or a State is a party)`

-- s 64: "In any suit to which the Commonwealth or a State is a party, the rights of parties shall as nearly as possible be the
-- same, and judgment may be given and costs awarded on either side, as in a suit between subject and subject."
-- Outside such a suit s 64 is silent (a scope limit with no "only"): NOTHING.
@export I am in a suit to which the Commonwealth or a State is a party. Are my rights as nearly as possible the same as in a suit against a private person?
GIVEN `the Commonwealth or a State is a party to my suit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commonwealth or a State (not a Territory) is a party to your suit, as plaintiff or defendant
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Are my rights as against a private person question` MEANS
    IF `the Commonwealth or a State is a party to my suit`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

@export I am in a suit to which the Commonwealth or a State is a party. Can the court give judgment and award costs for or against me, as between private persons?
GIVEN `the Commonwealth or a State is a party to my suit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commonwealth or a State (not a Territory) is a party to your suit, as plaintiff or defendant
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can costs be awarded on either side question` MEANS
    IF `the Commonwealth or a State is a party to my suit`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 79(1), (1A)(b), s 80A -- which State's or Territory's law binds the court in my suit`

-- s 79(1): "The laws of each State or Territory, including the laws relating to procedure, evidence, and the competency of
-- witnesses, shall, except as otherwise provided by the Constitution or the laws of the Commonwealth, be binding on all Courts
-- exercising federal jurisdiction in that State or Territory in all cases to which they are applicable."
-- s 79(1A)(b): the court is taken to exercise federal jurisdiction "(i) if the proceeding is transferred or remitted -- [in] the
-- State or Territory to which the proceeding is transferred or remitted; and (ii) otherwise -- the State or Territory in which
-- the proceeding is commenced". (s 79(1A)(a), the Federal Court's indictable proceedings under s 68C(5), is criminal and is
-- not reached from this seat.)
-- s 80A: ss 79 and 80 "apply, in so far as they are capable of application, to and in relation to a court of a Territory
-- exercising jurisdiction in a suit brought by virtue of section 56 or Part IXA."
DECLARE `Law that binds the court` IS ONE OF
    `the laws of that State or Territory, including procedure, evidence and the competency of witnesses, so far as they are applicable (s 79(1))` HAS `whose` IS A `Place`
    `on this point, the Commonwealth law or the Constitution that otherwise provides (s 79(1)); on everything else, the laws of that State or Territory so far as applicable` HAS `whose` IS A `Place`

GIVEN `where my suit was commenced` IS A `Place`
      `my suit was transferred or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `where it was transferred or remitted to` IS A `Place`
GIVETH A `Place`
`the place whose law binds the court in my suit` MEANS
    IF `my suit was transferred or remitted`
    THEN `where it was transferred or remitted to`
    ELSE `where my suit was commenced`

@export My civil suit is in a court exercising federal jurisdiction (for example against the Commonwealth in a State court), or in a Territory court under s 56. Which State's or Territory's law binds the court?
GIVEN `the court is exercising federal jurisdiction in my suit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is exercising federal jurisdiction (it is, for example, in a suit against the Commonwealth in a State court, or where the claim arises under a Commonwealth law or the Constitution)
      `the court is a Territory court hearing my suit against the Commonwealth under s 56` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your suit is against the Commonwealth, brought under s 56 of the Judiciary Act, in a court of a Territory
      `where my suit was commenced` IS A `Place` @desc The State or Territory in which your suit was started
      `my suit was transferred or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your suit has been transferred or remitted to a court in another place
      `where it was transferred or remitted to` IS A `Place` @desc If transferred or remitted, the State or Territory it went to (otherwise any value; it is not read)
      `a Commonwealth law or the Constitution makes its own provision on the point` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Commonwealth law (such as the Evidence Act 1995 where it applies) or the Constitution itself deals with the point in question
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Law that binds the court`
`Which law binds the court in my suit question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the court is exercising federal jurisdiction in my suit`) AND (NOT `the court is a Territory court hearing my suit against the Commonwealth under s 56`)
    THEN `not covered` `the court is not exercising federal jurisdiction, and it is not a Territory court hearing a s 56 suit, so s 79 does not apply; the law of the place applies of its own force`
    ELSE IF `a Commonwealth law or the Constitution makes its own provision on the point`
         THEN `answered` (`on this point, the Commonwealth law or the Constitution that otherwise provides (s 79(1)); on everything else, the laws of that State or Territory so far as applicable` (`the place whose law binds the court in my suit` `where my suit was commenced` `my suit was transferred or remitted` `where it was transferred or remitted to`))
         ELSE `answered` (`the laws of that State or Territory, including procedure, evidence and the competency of witnesses, so far as they are applicable (s 79(1))` (`the place whose law binds the court in my suit` `where my suit was commenced` `my suit was transferred or remitted` `where it was transferred or remitted to`))

§§ `s 79(2)-(4) -- can a State time limit or notice rule apply to my suit to recover an invalid State tax (if you paid such a tax)`

-- s 79(2): "A provision of this Act does not prevent a law of a State or Territory covered by subsection (3) from binding a court
-- under this section in connection with a suit relating to the recovery of an amount paid in connection with a tax that a law of
-- a State or Territory invalidly purported to impose." s 79(3): "This subsection covers a law of a State or Territory that would
-- be applicable to the suit if it did not involve federal jurisdiction, including, for example, a law ... (a) limiting the period
-- for bringing the suit to recover the amount; (b) requiring prior notice to be given to the person against whom the suit is
-- brought; (c) barring the suit on the grounds that the person bringing the suit has charged someone else for the amount."
-- s 79(4) gives examples of "an amount paid in connection with a tax" (the tax; a late-payment penalty; an underpayment
-- penalty; an amount a customer paid the taxpayer that is directly referable to the tax). Examples only: asked as one fact.
-- JUST TRUE: nothing in the Judiciary Act stops the State law binding the court. JUST FALSE: the State law is not one s 79(3)
-- covers, so s 79(2) does not save it. NOTHING: not a suit of this kind; s 79(2) is silent.
@export I am suing to get back money I paid under a State or Territory tax law that was invalid. Can that State's time limit, prior-notice rule or "passed on to customers" bar apply to my suit even though it is in federal jurisdiction?
GIVEN `my suit is to recover an amount paid in connection with an invalid State or Territory tax` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your suit is to recover an amount paid in connection with a tax that a State or Territory law invalidly purported to impose (the tax itself, a penalty for paying late or too little, or an amount your customers paid you that is directly referable to the tax)
      `the State law would apply to my suit if it were not in federal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State or Territory law in question (for example its time limit for such suits, a requirement to give notice first, or a bar where you passed the tax on to someone else) would apply to your suit if the suit did not involve federal jurisdiction
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a State limit bind my invalid tax suit question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my suit is to recover an amount paid in connection with an invalid State or Territory tax`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the State law would apply to my suit if it were not in federal jurisdiction`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 39B -- which court can I ask for mandamus, prohibition or an injunction against a Commonwealth officer`

-- s 39B(1): the Federal Court's original jurisdiction "includes jurisdiction with respect to any matter in which a writ of
-- mandamus or prohibition or an injunction is sought against an officer or officers of the Commonwealth", subject to (1B),
-- (1C) and (1EA). (1B): a decision to prosecute made by a Commonwealth officer, prosecution proposed in a State or Territory
-- court: not the Federal Court; the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the prosecution is proposed. (1C): while a
-- prosecution (or an appeal from it) is before a State or Territory court, a "related criminal justice process decision": not
-- the Federal Court; the Supreme Court of that State or Territory. (1D): (1C) does not apply if I applied before the
-- prosecution commenced. (1EA): while a civil proceeding is before the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia or a State
-- or Territory court, a "related civil proceeding decision" (an Attorney-General's or appointed Minister's notice or certificate
-- under the National Security Information (Criminal and Civil Proceedings) Act 2004): not the Federal Court; Division 1,
-- Division 2 or the Supreme Court of the State or Territory, as the case is. (2): a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family
-- Court of Australia (Division 1) is not an "officer of the Commonwealth" in (1), (1B), (1C) or (1D).
-- The High Court's own jurisdiction (Constitution s 75(v), and s 38(e) of this Act making it exclusive of State courts) is not
-- worked out here: the Constitution is not deposited.
DECLARE `The decision I want reviewed` IS ONE OF
    `a Commonwealth officer's decision to prosecute me, with the prosecution proposed in a State or Territory court`
    `another decision in the criminal process for an offence I am being prosecuted for (or appealing) in a State or Territory court`
    `a national security notice or certificate given in a civil case of mine before the Federal Circuit and Family Court or a State or Territory court`
    `any other decision or conduct of a Commonwealth officer`

DECLARE `Where my civil case is` IS ONE OF
    `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)`
    `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2)`
    `a court of a State or Territory`

DECLARE `Court for review of a Commonwealth officer` IS ONE OF
    `the Federal Court of Australia (s 39B(1))`
    `the Federal Court of Australia, because I applied before the prosecution began (s 39B(1D)); the prosecutor may apply for a permanent stay (s 39B(1E))`
    `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the prosecution is proposed (s 39B(1B))`
    `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the prosecution or appeal is before a court (s 39B(1C))`
    `not the Federal Court: the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) (s 39B(1EA))`
    `not the Federal Court: the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (s 39B(1EA))`
    `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the civil case is (s 39B(1EA))`

@export I want a court to order a Commonwealth officer to do (or stop doing) something, by mandamus, prohibition or an injunction. Can I go to the Federal Court, or must I go elsewhere?
GIVEN `the officer is a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family Court (Division 1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person whose decision you challenge is a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)
      `the decision I want reviewed` IS A `The decision I want reviewed` @desc What you want reviewed: a decision to prosecute you; another decision in a prosecution of you that is now before a State or Territory court; a national security notice or certificate in a civil case of yours; or any other decision or conduct
      `I applied before the prosecution began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a decision in a prosecution of you: whether you applied for the writ or injunction before the prosecution was started (otherwise any value; it is not read)
      `where my civil case is` IS A `Where my civil case is` @desc For a national security notice or certificate: which court your civil case (or the appeal in it) is before (otherwise any value; it is not read)
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Court for review of a Commonwealth officer`
`Which court can review a Commonwealth officer question` MEANS
    IF `the officer is a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family Court (Division 1)`
       AND (NOT (`the decision I want reviewed` EQUALS `a national security notice or certificate given in a civil case of mine before the Federal Circuit and Family Court or a State or Territory court`))
    THEN `not covered` `a Judge of the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) is not an "officer of the Commonwealth" for s 39B(1) and (1B)-(1D); the route to review a Judge's decision is not encoded here`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the decision I want reviewed`
         WHEN `a Commonwealth officer's decision to prosecute me, with the prosecution proposed in a State or Territory court`
              THEN `answered` `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the prosecution is proposed (s 39B(1B))`
         WHEN `another decision in the criminal process for an offence I am being prosecuted for (or appealing) in a State or Territory court`
              THEN IF `I applied before the prosecution began`
                   THEN `answered` `the Federal Court of Australia, because I applied before the prosecution began (s 39B(1D)); the prosecutor may apply for a permanent stay (s 39B(1E))`
                   ELSE `answered` `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the prosecution or appeal is before a court (s 39B(1C))`
         WHEN `a national security notice or certificate given in a civil case of mine before the Federal Circuit and Family Court or a State or Territory court`
              THEN CONSIDER `where my civil case is`
                   WHEN `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1)` THEN `answered` `not the Federal Court: the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) (s 39B(1EA))`
                   WHEN `the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2)` THEN `answered` `not the Federal Court: the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) (s 39B(1EA))`
                   WHEN `a court of a State or Territory` THEN `answered` `not the Federal Court: the Supreme Court of the State or Territory where the civil case is (s 39B(1EA))`
         WHEN `any other decision or conduct of a Commonwealth officer`
              THEN `answered` `the Federal Court of Australia (s 39B(1))`

-- s 39B(1E): "Where subsection (1D) applies, the prosecutor may apply to the court for a permanent stay of the proceedings
-- referred to in that subsection, and the court may grant such a stay if the court determines that: (a) the matters the subject
-- of the proceedings are more appropriately dealt with in the criminal justice process; and (b) a stay of proceedings will not
-- substantially prejudice the person." The court's determinations are inputs. JUST TRUE: the court may stay my case. JUST
-- FALSE: (1D) does not apply ("where"), the prosecutor has not applied, or a determination is not made ("if").
@export I applied to the Federal Court for review of a decision in my prosecution before the prosecution began. Can my Federal Court case be permanently stayed?
GIVEN `I applied before the prosecution began` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied to the Federal Court for mandamus, prohibition or an injunction about a decision in the criminal process before the prosecution against you was started
      `the prosecutor has applied for a permanent stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecutor has applied to the court to stay your case permanently
      `the court determines the matters are better dealt with in the criminal process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has determined that the matters in your case are more appropriately dealt with in the criminal justice process
      `the court determines a stay will not substantially prejudice me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has determined that a stay would not substantially prejudice you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my Federal Court review case be stayed question` MEANS
    IF `I applied before the prosecution began`
       AND `the prosecutor has applied for a permanent stay`
       AND `the court determines the matters are better dealt with in the criminal process`
       AND `the court determines a stay will not substantially prejudice me`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 78AA, 78B -- a constitutional point in my case (if your case raises one)`

-- s 78B(1): "Where a cause pending in a federal court including the High Court or in a court of a State or Territory involves a
-- matter arising under the Constitution or involving its interpretation, it is the duty of the court not to proceed in the cause
-- unless and until the court is satisfied that notice of the cause, specifying the nature of the matter has been given to the
-- Attorneys-General of the Commonwealth and of the States, and a reasonable time has elapsed since the giving of the notice for
-- consideration by the Attorneys-General, of the question of intervention in the proceedings or removal of the cause to the High
-- Court." s 78B(2)(c): the court "may continue to hear evidence and argument concerning matters severable from" the
-- constitutional matter. s 78B(5): "Nothing in subsection (1) prevents a court from proceeding without delay to hear and
-- determine proceedings, so far as they relate to the grant of urgent relief of an interlocutory nature, where the court thinks
-- it necessary in the interests of justice to do so."
-- JUST TRUE: the court must not go on with this step yet. JUST FALSE: no constitutional matter ("where"); the court is satisfied
-- of the notice and the time (FORK: the "unless and until" met answers JUST FALSE, the duty being spent; the other reading is
-- NOTHING); or the step is one s 78B(2)(c) or (5) lets the court take. NOTHING: my case is not in a court (a tribunal is not
-- named; a scope limit).
@export My case raises a point about the Constitution. Must the court hold off going on with my case until the Attorneys-General have been notified and have had time to consider intervening?
GIVEN `my case is in a court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is before a court (the High Court, another federal court, or a State or Territory court), not a tribunal
      `my case involves a constitutional matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case involves a matter arising under the Constitution or involving its interpretation
      `the court is satisfied notice has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that notice of your case, saying what the constitutional point is, has been given to the Attorneys-General who must get it
      `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has elapsed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that a reasonable time has passed since the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider intervening or having the case removed to the High Court
      `the step` IS A `Step in my case` @desc What the court would do now: grant urgent interlocutory relief it thinks necessary in the interests of justice; hear evidence and argument on matters separate from the constitutional point; or anything else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the court wait for the Attorneys-General question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my case is in a court`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF (NOT `my case involves a constitutional matter`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `the court is satisfied notice has been given` AND `the court is satisfied a reasonable time has elapsed`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE IF `the step` EQUALS `anything else (go on with the case)`
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 78B(1) as a duty on the court, with s 78B(2)(c) and (5): the court must not take a step that is neither urgent interlocutory
-- relief nor severable evidence and argument unless satisfied of both the notice and the reasonable time. A duty kept by not
-- being broken, so a prohibition with no deadline.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 78B(1) -- the court must not proceed until the Attorneys-General have had notice and a reasonable time` MEANS
    PARTY `the court`
    SHANT `proceed in the cause` notice elapsed step PROVIDED ((NOT (notice AND elapsed)) AND step EQUALS `anything else (go on with the case)`)
    LEST  BREACH BY `the court` BECAUSE "s 78B(1): the court must not proceed in a cause involving a constitutional matter until satisfied that the Attorneys-General have had notice and a reasonable time"

-- s 78B(1) "the Attorneys-General of the Commonwealth and of the States"; s 78AA: in this Division "State includes the
-- Australian Capital Territory and the Northern Territory"; s 78B(3)(b): notice "is not required to be given to the
-- Attorney-General of the Commonwealth if he or she or the Commonwealth is a party to the cause and is not required to be given
-- to the Attorney-General of a State if he or she or the State is a party to the cause."
DECLARE `Attorney-General` IS ONE OF
    `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth`
    `the Attorney-General of` HAS `whose` IS A `Place`

DECLARE `Government party to my case` IS ONE OF
    `no government is a party`
    `the Commonwealth (or its Attorney-General) is a party`
    `a State, the ACT or the NT (or its Attorney-General) is a party` HAS `which` IS A `Place`

-- The eight polities s 78AA makes "States" for s 78B, in the order s 78B's notice is usually addressed.
`the States for s 78B` MEANS
    LIST `New South Wales`, `Victoria`, `Queensland`, `Western Australia`, `South Australia`, `Tasmania`, `the Australian Capital Territory`, `the Northern Territory`

GIVEN `the party` IS A `Government party to my case`
      p IS A `Place`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `that State is a party` IF
    CONSIDER `the party`
    WHEN `a State, the ACT or the NT (or its Attorney-General) is a party` q THEN q EQUALS p
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the party` IS A `Government party to my case`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Attorney-General`
`the Commonwealth's Attorney-General, unless the Commonwealth is a party` MEANS
    IF `the party` EQUALS `the Commonwealth (or its Attorney-General) is a party`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE LIST `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth`

@export My case raises a constitutional point. Which Attorneys-General must be given notice of it?
GIVEN `the government party to my case` IS A `Government party to my case` @desc Whether a government is a party to your case: none; the Commonwealth (or its Attorney-General); or a State, the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory (or its Attorney-General), naming which
GIVETH A LIST OF `Attorney-General`
`Which Attorneys-General must get notice question` MEANS
    append (`the Commonwealth's Attorney-General, unless the Commonwealth is a party` `the government party to my case`)
           (map `the Attorney-General of` (filter (GIVEN p YIELD (NOT (`that State is a party` `the government party to my case` p))) `the States for s 78B`))

@export My case raises a constitutional point. How many Attorneys-General must be given notice of it?
GIVEN `the government party to my case` IS A `Government party to my case` @desc Whether a government is a party to your case: none; the Commonwealth (or its Attorney-General); or a State, the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory (or its Attorney-General), naming which
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many Attorneys-General must get notice question` MEANS
    count (`Which Attorneys-General must get notice question` `the government party to my case`)

§§ `ss 77MA, 77N -- interest in the High Court (if you are suing for money in the High Court)`

-- s 77MA commenced on 22 November 1984: inserted by the Statute Law (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act (No. 2) 1984 (No. 165, 1984,
-- assented to 25 October 1984), s 3, commencing "on the twenty-eighth day after the day on which it receives the Royal Assent"
-- (endnote 3, note (g)). A cause of action arising on that day arises after the commencement (an Act commences at the start of
-- its day).
`s 77MA commencement` MEANS Date 22 11 1984

-- s 77MA(1): "In any proceedings, other than proceedings on appeal, for the recovery of any money (including any debt or damages
-- or the value of any goods) in respect of a cause of action that arises after the commencement of this section, the High Court
-- shall, upon application, unless good cause is shown to the contrary, either: (a) order that there be included in the sum for
-- which judgment is given interest at such rate as the Court thinks fit ... between the date when the cause of action arose and
-- the date as of which judgment is entered; or (b) ... a lump sum in lieu of any such interest." (2)(b): it does not "apply in
-- relation to any debt upon which interest is payable as of right whether by virtue of an agreement or otherwise"; (2)(e): it
-- does not "authorize the giving of interest ... otherwise than by consent, upon any sum for which judgment is given by consent".
-- The rate is the Court's ("as the Court thinks fit"); not computed.
-- JUST TRUE: the High Court must include interest or a lump sum. JUST FALSE: an appeal (FORK: "other than proceedings on appeal"
-- read as a no; the other reading is NOTHING); no application ("upon", read as "where"); good cause shown ("unless"); a debt with
-- interest as of right ((2)(b), "does not apply"); a consent judgment without consent to interest ((2)(e)). NOTHING: not the
-- High Court, not a claim for money, or a cause of action that arose before 22 November 1984 (scope limits).
@export I am suing for money (a debt, damages or the value of goods) in the High Court. Must the Court add interest up to judgment (or a lump sum instead) if I ask for it?
GIVEN `my proceedings are in the High Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings are in the High Court of Australia
      `my proceedings are an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings are an appeal (including an application for a new trial)
      `my proceedings are to recover money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are suing to recover money: a debt, damages, or the value of goods
      `the date my cause of action arose` IS A DATE @desc The date your cause of action arose (for example the date of the injury, or of the breach of contract)
      `I have applied for interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Court for interest up to judgment
      `good cause has been shown against interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds that good cause has been shown why interest should not be given
      `the debt carries interest as of right` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the debt you claim already carries interest as of right, under an agreement or otherwise
      `the judgment is by consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment is being given by consent of the parties
      `the parties consent to interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a consent judgment: whether the parties have consented to interest being included (otherwise any value; it is not read)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the High Court give interest up to judgment question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my proceedings are in the High Court`)
       OR (NOT `my proceedings are to recover money`)
       OR (`the date my cause of action arose` LESS THAN `s 77MA commencement`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `my proceedings are an appeal`
              OR `the debt carries interest as of right`
              OR (NOT `I have applied for interest`)
              OR `good cause has been shown against interest`
              OR (`the judgment is by consent` AND (NOT `the parties consent to interest`))
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 77MA(2)(a): no "interest upon interest"; (3): no interest on any amount for "(a) compensation in respect of liabilities
-- incurred which do not carry interest as against the person claiming interest ...; (b) compensation for loss or damage to be
-- incurred or suffered after the date on which judgment is given; or (c) exemplary or punitive damages"; (4): but interest may be
-- given on a (3)(a) liability "where that liability has been met by the plaintiff, as from the date upon which that liability
-- was so met". The answer is the most the Court can give interest on; it "may" give it on "the whole or any part".
-- Amounts in dollars; a negative remainder is read as nil.
@export The High Court is giving me judgment for money. On how much of it can interest up to judgment be given?
GIVEN `the sum for which judgment is given` IS A NUMBER @desc The total sum of your judgment, in dollars
      `the part that is itself interest` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of that sum is already interest (no interest is given on interest), in dollars
      `the part for future loss` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the sum is for loss or damage you will suffer after the judgment date (for example future medical costs or future lost earnings), in dollars
      `the part for exemplary or punitive damages` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the sum is exemplary or punitive damages, in dollars
      `the part for liabilities I have not yet paid` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the sum compensates you for liabilities you have incurred, that carry no interest against you, and that you have not yet paid (for example an unpaid hospital bill), in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can carry interest up to judgment question` MEANS
    max 0 (`the sum for which judgment is given` MINUS `the parts that cannot carry interest`)
    WHERE
        `the parts that cannot carry interest` MEANS
            `the part that is itself interest` PLUS `the part for future loss` PLUS `the part for exemplary or punitive damages` PLUS `the part for liabilities I have not yet paid`

-- s 77MA(1)(a): interest runs "between the date when the cause of action arose and the date as of which judgment is entered";
-- s 77MA(4): on a (3)(a) liability the plaintiff has met, "as from the date upon which that liability was so met".
@export The High Court is giving me interest up to judgment. From what date can interest run on a particular part of my claim?
GIVEN `the date my cause of action arose` IS A DATE @desc The date your cause of action arose
      `this part compensates a liability I have paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this part of your claim compensates you for a liability you incurred that carries no interest against you (such as a medical bill), and that you have since paid
      `the date I paid it` IS A DATE @desc If so, the date you paid that liability (otherwise any date; it is not read)
GIVETH A DATE
`From when can interest up to judgment run question` MEANS
    IF `this part compensates a liability I have paid`
    THEN `the date I paid it`
    ELSE `the date my cause of action arose`

-- s 77N: "A judgment debt under a judgment of the High Court carries interest, at such rate as is fixed by the Rules of Court,
-- from the date as of which the judgment is entered." The Rules of Court are not deposited; the rate is not given.
-- NOTHING for a judgment of another court (a scope limit; that court's own law governs).
@export I have a High Court judgment for money. From what date does the judgment debt carry interest?
GIVEN `my judgment is a High Court judgment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your judgment was given by the High Court of Australia
      `the date the judgment is entered` IS A DATE @desc The date as of which the judgment is entered
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`From when does my High Court judgment debt carry interest question` MEANS
    IF `my judgment is a High Court judgment`
    THEN JUST `the date the judgment is entered`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `s 44(1), (2A) -- can my High Court suit be sent to another court (if you have sued in the High Court)`

-- s 44(1): a matter pending in the High Court "may, upon the application of a party or of the High Court's own motion, be remitted
-- by the High Court to any federal court, court of a State or court of a Territory that has jurisdiction with respect to the
-- subject-matter and the parties". (2A): "Where a matter in which the Commonwealth ... is a party is at any time pending in the
-- High Court, the High Court may ... remit the matter, or any part of the matter, to the Federal Court of Australia." (3): that
-- court then has jurisdiction. s 44(2) (matters in s 38(a)-(d)) is not reached from this seat except a treaty matter, and is out.
DECLARE `Where my High Court suit can be remitted` IS ONE OF
    `to the Federal Court (s 44(2A)), or to any federal, State or Territory court with jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties (s 44(1))`
    `to the Federal Court (s 44(2A)), which then has jurisdiction (s 44(3)); no other court has jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties`
    `to any federal, State or Territory court with jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties (s 44(1))`

-- NOTHING where neither subsection reaches the case: s 44(1) limits itself to a court with jurisdiction, in its own words, without
-- "only".
@export I have sued in the High Court. Can the High Court send my case (or part of it) to another court, and to which?
GIVEN `the Commonwealth is a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commonwealth (or a person suing or being sued on its behalf) is a party to your High Court case
      `another court has jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether some other federal, State or Territory court could deal with this kind of claim between these parties
GIVETH A MAYBE `Where my High Court suit can be remitted`
`Where can my High Court suit be remitted question` MEANS
    IF `the Commonwealth is a party` AND `another court has jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties`
    THEN JUST `to the Federal Court (s 44(2A)), or to any federal, State or Territory court with jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties (s 44(1))`
    ELSE IF `the Commonwealth is a party`
         THEN JUST `to the Federal Court (s 44(2A)), which then has jurisdiction (s 44(3)); no other court has jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties`
         ELSE IF `another court has jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties`
              THEN JUST `to any federal, State or Territory court with jurisdiction over the subject-matter and the parties (s 44(1))`
              ELSE NOTHING

§§ `ss 65, 66, 67E -- getting paid when I win against the Commonwealth or a State (if you have a judgment against a government)`

-- s 65: "No execution or attachment, or process in the nature thereof, shall be issued against the property or revenues of the
-- Commonwealth or a State in any such suit; but when any judgment is given against the Commonwealth or a State, the Registrar or
-- other appropriate officer shall give to the party in whose favour the judgment is given a certificate in the form of the
-- Schedule, or to a like effect." s 67E: "No execution or attachment, or process in the nature thereof, shall be issued against
-- the property or moneys of the Territory" (the Northern Territory, s 67A).
-- JUST FALSE: a flat negative. NOTHING: another Territory, or a private debtor (the Act is silent).
@export I have a money judgment against the Commonwealth, a State or the Northern Territory. Can I enforce it by execution or attachment against the government's property?
GIVEN `the government I have judgment against` IS A `Government` @desc The government your judgment is against: the Commonwealth, or the government of a State or Territory (name which)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I execute against the government question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the government I have judgment against`
    WHEN `the Commonwealth` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `the government of` p THEN
        IF `is one of the six States` p OR p EQUALS `the Northern Territory`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 66: "On receipt of the certificate of a judgment against the Commonwealth or a State the Minister for Finance or the
-- Treasurer of the State as the case may be shall satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available."
DECLARE `How my judgment is paid` IS ONE OF
    `the Registrar gives me a certificate of the judgment (s 65, the Schedule form); on receiving it, the Minister for Finance must satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available (s 66)`
    `the Registrar gives me a certificate of the judgment (s 65, the Schedule form); on receiving it, the Treasurer of that State must satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available (s 66)` HAS `which State` IS A `Place`

@export I have won a money judgment against the Commonwealth or a State. How do I get paid?
GIVEN `the government I have judgment against` IS A `Government` @desc The government your judgment is against: the Commonwealth, or the government of a State or Territory (name which)
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `How my judgment is paid`
`How is my judgment against a government paid question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the government I have judgment against`
    WHEN `the Commonwealth` THEN `answered` `the Registrar gives me a certificate of the judgment (s 65, the Schedule form); on receiving it, the Minister for Finance must satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available (s 66)`
    WHEN `the government of` p THEN
        IF `is one of the six States` p
        THEN `answered` (`the Registrar gives me a certificate of the judgment (s 65, the Schedule form); on receiving it, the Treasurer of that State must satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available (s 66)` p)
        ELSE `not covered` `the government party is a Territory, not the Commonwealth or a State; ss 63, 65 and 66 name only the Commonwealth and the States`

-- s 65 as a prohibition on me: I must not issue execution or attachment against the government's property or revenues.
GIVEN `the government I have judgment against` IS A `Government`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 65 -- I must not issue execution against the property or revenues of the Commonwealth or a State` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the claimant`
    SHANT `issue execution or attachment against the property or revenues of` (EXACTLY `the government I have judgment against`)
    LEST  BREACH BY `I, the claimant` BECAUSE "s 65: no execution or attachment shall be issued against the property or revenues of the Commonwealth or a State"

-- ss 65, 66 as a chain: the Registrar must give me the certificate; on its receipt, the Minister for Finance (or the State's
-- Treasurer) must satisfy the judgment. Neither duty has a time limit in the Act, so neither rule has a WITHIN.
GIVEN `the payer` IS A `Claim party`
      `the judgment sum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`ss 65, 66 -- the Registrar must certify my judgment and the government must then satisfy it` MEANS
    PARTY `the Registrar or other appropriate officer`
    MUST  `give me a certificate of the judgment`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the payer`
        MUST  `satisfy the judgment out of moneys legally available` (EXACTLY `the judgment sum`)

GIVEN `the government I have judgment against` IS A `Government`
GIVETH A `Claim party`
`who satisfies a judgment against` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the government I have judgment against`
    WHEN `the Commonwealth` THEN `the Minister for Finance`
    OTHERWISE `the Treasurer of the State`

§§ `ss 67, 82, 83 -- when the Commonwealth sues me (if the Commonwealth or a State is suing you)`

-- s 67: "When in any such suit a judgment is given in favour of the Commonwealth or of a State and against any person, the
-- Commonwealth or the State, as the case may be, may enforce the judgment against that person by process of extent, or by such
-- execution, attachment, or other process as could be had in a suit between subject and subject."
-- JUST TRUE for the Commonwealth or a State; NOTHING for a Territory (s 67 is silent).
@export The Commonwealth or a State has a judgment against me. Can it enforce it against me, by process of extent or the ordinary means of execution?
GIVEN `the government with judgment against me` IS A `Government` @desc The government that has the judgment against you: the Commonwealth, or the government of a State or Territory (name which)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the government enforce its judgment against me question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the government with judgment against me`
    WHEN `the Commonwealth` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the government of` p THEN
        IF `is one of the six States` p
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING

-- s 82: "Suits to recover pecuniary penalties and forfeitures under the laws of the Commonwealth may be brought either in the
-- State or Territory where they accrue or in the State or Territory where the offender is found." s 83: "Suits to recover taxes
-- accruing under any revenue law of the Commonwealth may be brought either in the State or Territory where the liability for
-- the tax occurs or in the State or Territory where the debtor resides." The answer lists the places (once, if they are the
-- same).
DECLARE `What the Commonwealth is suing me for` IS ONE OF
    `a pecuniary penalty or forfeiture under a Commonwealth law`
    `a tax under a Commonwealth revenue law`

@export The Commonwealth is suing me for a civil penalty, a forfeiture or a tax. In which States or Territories can it bring the suit?
GIVEN `what the Commonwealth is suing me for` IS A `What the Commonwealth is suing me for` @desc Whether the suit is for a pecuniary penalty or forfeiture under a Commonwealth law, or for a tax under a Commonwealth revenue law
      `where the penalty accrued or the tax liability occurs` IS A `Place` @desc The State or Territory where the penalty or forfeiture accrued, or where the liability for the tax occurs
      `where I am found or reside` IS A `Place` @desc For a penalty or forfeiture: the State or Territory where you are found; for a tax: the State or Territory where you reside
GIVETH A LIST OF `Place`
`Where can the Commonwealth sue me question` MEANS
    IF `where the penalty accrued or the tax liability occurs` EQUALS `where I am found or reside`
    THEN LIST `where the penalty accrued or the tax liability occurs`
    ELSE LIST `where the penalty accrued or the tax liability occurs`, `where I am found or reside`

§§ `ss 55ZG(3), 55ZI -- acts done under a Legal Services Direction (if your claim concerns how the Commonwealth ran its legal work)`

-- s 55ZI: "(1) The Attorney-General is not liable to an action or other proceeding, whether civil or criminal, for or in relation
-- to an act done or omitted to be done in compliance, or purported compliance, with a Legal Services Direction. (2) A person
-- (other than the Attorney-General) is not liable to an action or other proceeding, whether civil or criminal, for or in
-- relation to an act done or omitted to be done by the person in compliance, or in good faith in purported compliance, with a
-- Legal Services Direction."
-- JUST FALSE: a flat negative ("is not liable"). NOTHING: the act was not in compliance (or, for a person other than the
-- Attorney-General, not in good-faith purported compliance); s 55ZI is then silent.
DECLARE `How the act relates to the Direction` IS ONE OF
    `done in compliance with a Legal Services Direction`
    `done in purported compliance, in good faith`
    `done in purported compliance, not in good faith`
    `not done under a Legal Services Direction`

@export I want to sue someone over something done (or not done) in the Commonwealth's legal work. Can they be liable to my action if it was done under a Legal Services Direction?
GIVEN `the person I want to sue is the Attorney-General` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you want to sue is the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth
      `how the act relates to the Direction` IS A `How the act relates to the Direction` @desc Whether the act or omission was done in compliance with a Legal Services Direction, in good-faith purported compliance, in purported compliance but not in good faith, or not under a Direction at all
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can they be liable for acting under a Legal Services Direction question` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how the act relates to the Direction`
    WHEN `done in compliance with a Legal Services Direction` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `done in purported compliance, in good faith` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `done in purported compliance, not in good faith` THEN
        IF `the person I want to sue is the Attorney-General`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING
    WHEN `not done under a Legal Services Direction` THEN NOTHING

-- s 55ZG(3): "The issue of non-compliance with a Legal Services Direction may not be raised in any proceeding (whether in a
-- court, tribunal or other body) except by, or on behalf of, the Commonwealth." JUST FALSE for me (a flat negative); JUST TRUE
-- only if I act for the Commonwealth.
@export The Commonwealth's lawyers did not follow a Legal Services Direction in my case. Can I raise that in my proceeding?
GIVEN `I act for the Commonwealth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the Commonwealth or are acting on its behalf in the proceeding (for a private claimant: no)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I raise non-compliance with a Legal Services Direction question` MEANS
    IF `I act for the Commonwealth`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
